# Secrets — never commit
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
.uv-python/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/

# Node / Next.js
node_modules/
.next/
out/
.turbo/

# Terraform
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example

# Local artefacts
*.log
*.err
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
/tmp/
coverage/
.coverage
# What the suite that produced `.coverage` did (scripts/check_coverage_ratchet.py). An
# artefact of ONE run on ONE machine, like the data file it sits beside: committing it
# would hand the gate a manifest describing somebody else's run.
.coverage-run.json
htmlcov/

# Engine verification / eval outputs are committed deliberately, not by accident
!docs/**

# Local Redis snapshot — a runtime artifact of the dev redis-server, never source.
dump.rdb

# What `scripts/vps-deploy.sh` remembers about a HOST: the SHA last deployed there and
# the deploy history. Per-host state, not source — committing it would make one machine's
# history the whole fleet's, and the script's change detection would then diff against
# whatever the last committer's box happened to be running.
.deploy-state/


# Agent worktrees. `.claude/worktrees/<id>/` is a FULL working copy of this repository,
# created per background agent and removed when it finishes — committing one would nest
# the whole tree inside itself, and the copies are transient by construction.
#
# Scoped to `worktrees/` rather than all of `.claude/` on purpose: agent definitions and
# project settings under `.claude/` are the kind of thing a repo legitimately checks in,
# and a blanket ignore would silently swallow them the day somebody adds one.
.claude/worktrees/

# Live production credentials — never commit (see the file's own header)
VPS_INPUTS.md

# The coverage ratchet's probe package — a THREE-LINE FILE THAT BROKE CI FOR AN HOUR.
#
# `coverage_ratchet_guard_test` proves the unguarded-surface check against the REAL tree
# rather than a fixture (a fixture would prove only that the fixture parses), so it writes
# `apps/api/_ratchet_probe/models.py`, asserts the checker names it, and removes it in a
# `finally`. The cleanup is correct. What is not safe is `git add -A` while that test is
# running: the probe gets committed, and from then on it is a permanent second writer of
# an append-only table in a package no area guards — so `unguarded_surfaces()` reports it
# forever, `test_the_real_tree_leaves_no_hard_rule_surface_unguarded` fails, the probe test
# fails on its own "must not collide with real code" precondition, and the ratchet REFUSES
# TO SCORE. That is exactly what happened in commit 367270a.
#
# Ignoring it is the durable fix: the test writes and reads the path directly, so it is
# unaffected, and no `add -A` can ever capture it again.
apps/api/_ratchet_probe/
